Hello,

I bet this gets asked pretty often on this list, but in the archives the =
latest thread I can find about this is dated in 2002. So here goes:

What is the best choice as far as distributions go for Alpha right now?

I have found three choices (there are probably more, that's why I ask):

- Red Hat 7.2 (from Compaq/HP - =
http://h18000.www1.hp.com/products/software/alpha-tools/redhat/)
- Debian 3.0 (Woody)
- Suse 8.1

I'm a Red Hat guy myself (I'm moving my current x86 Red Hat 7.2 system =
to this Alpha machine), but I think it's a shame that Red Hat has =
stopped updating the Alpha version of its distro. So I'm wondering, in =
the even I go with Red Hat, if I'll have trouble getting security =
updates, RPMs of software, etc.

OTOH, I've already tried Debian on that machine (I had a bit of time =
before my new/bigger hard drive arrived, so I thought I could try =
something else while I waited, even if I had to wipe when it arrived), =
and I didn't really like it. I was kind of overwhelmed by the sheer =
number of packages available in dselect, though the dependency =
management is great. Also, it didn't seem that the packages were updated =
very often, although I admit I always stayed in the Stable tree.

And I've never tried Suse. How's that? What are the chief differences =
between that and Red Hat?

I know this list is hosted by Red Hat, so there may be a bias in the =
answers. But as I said, I've been using Red Hat, so I won't really mind =
going back to it, as long as the questions raised above don't lead me to =
a dead-end system...

Thanks in advance,

J-S

