> Hello,
>
> I bet this gets asked pretty often on this list, but in the archives the
> latest thread I can find about this is dated in 2002. So here goes:
>
> What is the best choice as far as distributions go for Alpha right now?
>
> I have found three choices (there are probably more, that's why I ask):
>
> - Red Hat 7.2 (from Compaq/HP -
> http://h18000.www1.hp.com/products/software/alpha-tools/redhat/) - Debian
> 3.0 (Woody)
> - Suse 8.1
>
> I'm a Red Hat guy myself (I'm moving my current x86 Red Hat 7.2 system to
> this Alpha machine), but I think it's a shame that Red Hat has stopped
> updating the Alpha version of its distro. So I'm wondering, in the even I
> go with Red Hat, if I'll have trouble getting security updates, RPMs of
> software, etc.
>

On the downloads page at:

http://h18000.www1.hp.com/products/software/alpha-tools/redhat/download.html

you will find links to the update sites.  HP provides updated (s)rpm's for 
security updates and bug fixes.  The latest update was on Friday.

> OTOH, I've already tried Debian on that machine (I had a bit of time before
> my new/bigger hard drive arrived, so I thought I could try something else
> while I waited, even if I had to wipe when it arrived), and I didn't really
> like it. I was kind of overwhelmed by the sheer number of packages
> available in dselect, though the dependency management is great. Also, it
> didn't seem that the packages were updated very often, although I admit I
> always stayed in the Stable tree.
>
> And I've never tried Suse. How's that? What are the chief differences
> between that and Red Hat?

I lke SuSe. Its desktop is more modern that RH-7.2.  It appears to work fine.  
I gave it a try on an XP1000.  I do not know how well it works on other alpha 
systems, but I expect that it should be fine.  The SuSe bits are "as is" 
without any offical support from SuSe, though I suspect that if you had 
problems that you would find some help on the suse-axp@suse.com mailing list.  
I do not know the status of security updates.

>
> I know this list is hosted by Red Hat, so there may be a bias in the
> answers. But as I said, I've been using Red Hat, so I won't really mind
> going back to it, as long as the questions raised above don't lead me to a
> dead-end system...

The RH-7.2 bits are not as old as the version number would lead you to 
believe.  It is much closer to RH-7.3 than RH-7.2 and HP has provided over 
250 updated rpms, that keep things fairly current.  For example, the 2.4.18 
kernel.  

You can still purchase a one year support contract from HP for RH-7.2 for the 
Alpha Architrecutre.  If you purchase a contract then I suspect that you 
would be safe for at least one year. <evil grin>

If you want a stable and secure server system, then I would go with RH-7.2.  
If you are looking for a more modern desktop system,  then I would consider 
either SuSe or gentoo.

Best Regards,


--George




