Here's my 2 cents worth:

Although the support from HP/Compaq is excellent and ongoing, there just
doesn't seem to be a commitment for an Alpha future in the RedHat
distribution.  Continued kernel development efforts led by George France
and Jay Estabrook on the Alpha and security updates are helpful, but
there doesn't appear to be any movement towards new software releases
(e.g., Gnome2, etc.).

I haven't tried Suse, but a similar lack of future development for the
Alpha port has been announced.  The most recent release (8.1 I believe)
was done by Alpha enthusiasts in their spare time, and is not
supported.  I don't think you can count on continued Alpha release by
Suse, either.

There is no doubt about it, the money in linux distributions lies in the
x86 port; I understand that running a business requires certain
decisions to be made about market penetrance, etc., but that doesn't
help non-x86 architecture enthusiasts much.

The Debian group, however, have moved alpha in line with the other ports
that they support.  Although the stable release distribution tends to
lean in the direction of older, more stable code (e.g., compared to
RedHat), it is rock solid.  As new versions are released, I think you
can bet that Alpha ports will continue.  And of course, you can still
use a newer kernel with these distributions.

I used RedHat distributions for about ten years, but the situation with
the Alpha port (and, to a lesser extent, IA64) has prompted me to check
out Debian, and I find it excellent.  I admit that the first time I
tried to use dselect, I found the interface troublesome.  After
investing some time with it, however, I found that it works quite well.

There is a newer program called tasksel which allows you to install
related groups of packages with much less trouble.

The real key, IMHO, is to use apt-get to install packages that you need
(after you get a useful set of core packages using tasksel).  This
approach requires less time and effort (you completing automate
upgrades) than using the RedHat Network (and it is free).

One of the problems that I face in a mixed-architure workgroup
environment (Itanium I, Itanium II, Alpha ev6, ev5, ev56, Athlon, x86
machines) is dealing with version-dependent differences in software
(compilers, desktops, etc) and in spending time installing updates.  I
am a University professor with an active research program, but all
machines are on the internet, so these systems require constant
attention.

So, I am running the same version of Debian on all my machines
(currently woody, but I am testing the stability of sarge, the testing
distribution on a few machines).  I have automated the updating process
and am pleased with the results.

By the way, I have just installed Debian on four AlphaServer 2100A
quad-processor machines.  Since only ftp.kernel.org kernels on the
2.4.22 preview have incorporated the patches from the HP/Compaq group
required to support this new hardware, I had to muck around with the
installation tree to get the new kernel onto the installation CD.  Since
everything at Debian is documented (unlike the RedHat anacreon system)
this process wasn't too difficult.  I am grateful, however, to George
and Jay for taking the time to add support for these machines; the
latest updates to rh7.2-alpha support this architecture, but I had the
mixed-architecture problems mentioned above.

So, you might want to try Debian.  Haven't tried Gentoo yet. 

Similar problems exist for the IA64 version of RedHat.  Although I paid
$500 for a version of rh7.2 a little over a year ago

 
