On Sun, 03 Aug 2003 11:12:46 -0400
Jean-Sébastien Guay <jean_seb@videotron.ca> wrote:

> and I didn't really like it. I was kind of overwhelmed by the sheer
> number of packages available in dselect, though the dependency
> management is great. Also, it didn't seem that the packages were updated
> very often, although I admit I always stayed in the Stable tree.

Debian is a great Distro and it can't be jugded just by dselect. There
is kpackage, aptitude and some other replacements for dselect. If you
don't like woody because it's a little older, do a bare install of woody,
do not add any packages, edit /etc/apt/sources.list and replace stable
with testing or unstable. Then apt-get update and apt-get upgrade. You
will have a very stable and brand new Linux at hand despite it's name
testing or unstable... While working with Debian you will discover all
those little nice features that make this distro so much easier to
handle than others. 

Regards, Adrian.



