I'm trying to boot the RedHat 7.2 installer using the documentation
given in the installation instructions, appendix B (here:
http://h18000.www1.hp.com/products/software/alpha-tools/redhat/ig/ch-milo.html).


First thing I try is to boot from the CD (using a MILO diskette first of
course). My MILO command:

boot scd0:/kernels/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/scd0 initrd=/images/cdrom.img

(after correcting some typos from the docs and adding a root= option).
The error message I get, after the kernel and the initrd image seem to
load correctly and the kernel starts to load drivers is:

RAMDISK: Couldn't find valid RAM disk image at 0.
Freeing initrd memory: 2207k freed.
request_module[block-major-8]: Root fs not mounted
VFS: Cannot open root device "" or 08:02
Please append correct "root=" boot option.
Kernel panic: VFS: Unable to mount root fs on 08:02

I tried a couple other things, varying the root= option's setting (once
a hard drive partition, etc) and it always gave the same message.


I then tried another initrd image:

boot scd0:/kernels/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/scd0 initrd=/images/ramdisk.img

This, miraculously, boots me into the text mode installer. (The Debian
Alpha CD could not even boot directly from the CD, I needed 3 diskettes)
I was wondering if there was a way to boot into the graphical installer?
I've tried other images as the initrd, and they all give the Kernel
panic message except the ramdisk.img one.


Now, here are the other things I tried tonight before getting the CD to
boot into the text installer:

1. Tried booting from a diskette with generic.img rawwrited on it (and
another one with ramdisk.img, as per the diskette boot instructions).
Command: "boot fd0:/generic.img root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1" (which
didn't work) and then "boot fd0:". They both gave me an "unknown
filesystem on fd0" message from MILO...
2. Tried booting with a diskette with vmlinux.gz copied on it (and
another with ramdisk.img). Command: "boot fd0:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0
load_ramdisk=1". This gave me an "invalid compression scheme" message
after 5 '#' characters (progress bar)


I'll try installing from the text installer tomorrow, see how that goes.
But my first impression was a funny one... Apparently the default SCSI
adapter on Alphas, the NCRC8xx, is not supported by default by the
installer's kernel? I had to insert the "additional block drivers"
diskette to get the driver for that (lucky I had another machine handy
to make the diskette on the fly).

Thanks in advance,

J-S

