On Thursday 14 August 2003 12:32 pm, Uncle George wrote:
> Does anyone know what the deal is with the mysterious GPREL16
> stuff is!
>
> : relocation truncated to fit: GPREL16 .sbss
>
> ../unxlnga.pro/slo/fmgridif.o(.text+0x191f4): In function 
`__tcf_6':
> : relocation truncated to fit: GPREL16 .sbss
>
> ../unxlnga.pro/slo/deflt3d.o(.text+0x154): In function
>
> `E3dDefaultAttributes::Reset()':
> : relocation truncated to fit: GPREL16 .sdata
>
> ../unxlnga.pro/slo/deflt3d.o(.text+0x15c): In function
>
> `E3dDefaultAttributes::Reset()':
> : relocation truncated to fit: GPREL16 .sdata
>
> ../unxlnga.pro/slo/deflt3d.o(.text+0x21c): In function
>
> `E3dDefaultAttributes::Reset()':
> : relocation truncated to fit: GPREL16 .sdata
>
> collect2: ld returned 1 exit status

Hmmm.  AFAIK gcc shouldn't be generating GPREL16 symbols unless 
it's run with the -msmall-data option turned on, either 
explicitly or implicitly.  GPREL16 is a 16-bit GP-relative 
relocation, used to address static data (in the .sdata section).  
Using GPREL16 limits your static data area to 64K; using GPREL32 
(32-bit GP-relative relocation) limits your static data area to 
4GB.  The default is GPREL32, for obvious reasons.

Chances are, the source code is built with -msmall-data, or it's 
built with -fpic (which implies -msmall-data) instead of -fPIC 
(which implies -mlarge-data).  Then these errors are caused by 
having too much static data to fit in 64K.  If that's not the 
case, I would suspect it's a toolchain bug.

You can read up on these options in the gcc man page.

-- 
